# TidyMac validate loop (TM-MCP-01)

Spec: `../tidymac-docs/features/TM-MCP-01/spec.md`; Windows gates: `docs/windows/TM-WINDOWS-01.md`. Server: `crates/mcp` (`tidymac-mcp`), registered in `.mcp.json`. Build/reconnect only when the workspace's Windows toolchain and core support it.

## Safety (enforced by the server, never work around it)

- The real home is scanned **read-only** (`tidymac_scan` with `home: "real"`). Plan and clean of a real-home scan are refused; nothing enables them.
- Plan, clean, verify and history work only on sandbox fixtures from `tidymac_fixture_create`; moves go to the fixture's own `Trash/`.
- Do not clean real files by any other route either (no `make dev` clicks, no scripts against `$HOME`).
- For Windows, real cleanup is disabled until the handle-identity and Recycle Bin gates pass. Check that MCP tools are actually listed before describing a tool call as run; if not, continue with bounded local inspection and say MCP runtime validation is unavailable.
- If the MSYS2 MinGW-w64 gcc (`gcc.exe` from `<msys64>\mingw64\bin`) or the Windows SDK is missing, do not install/alter system setup without approval and do not claim that a Windows build passed. Report the exact blocker; run only checks that don't require it. The Tauri lib test binary cannot start on the GNU toolchain, so workspace test runs exclude `-p tidymac`.

## The loop

Note the time you start (`since`), then repeat the test-first fix-and-check cycle until every applicable gate passes:

1. Before changing behavior, add/update a focused regression or acceptance test. Run it first and confirm it fails for the intended reason (red).
2. Implement the smallest production change that makes the test pass (green), then refactor without changing behavior. A test that was added only after implementation is not TDD evidence.
3. Run `tidymac_run_checks {suite: "all"}`. Each failing step lists `failures[]` with `kind` (test, compile, lint, format, type, command), `name`, `file`, `line`, `message`, `output`.
4. Fix each failure using the same test-first loop. While iterating, run the narrow suite: `{suite: "core", filter: "templates::"}`, `{suite: "ui", filter: "flows"}`.
5. Run `tidymac_selftest`: junk_home fixture â†’ scan Cleanup â†’ plan default â†’ clean â†’ verify. On failure read `failed_step`, the step's `detail` and `log_lines`; the fixture is kept, so `tidymac_verify_fixture` / `tidymac_history` / `tidymac_scan_items` can dig in.
6. Smoke: `tidymac_scan {flow: {kind: "module", id: "cleanup"}, home: "real"}` (read-only). `needs_access` categories mean the host process lacks Full Disk Access; that is a finding to report, not a bug to fix.
7. `tidymac_logs_errors {since}`: warnings and errors grouped by event with the latest `hint`. Follow the hint; `tidymac_logs {event, scan_id|plan_id|run_id|fixture_id}` shows the lines.
8. Back to 3 until everything passes. Then `make check` once and record commands and results in `build/*-ledger.md`.

Follow the repository architecture rules in root `AGENTS.md`. Keep the modular monolith and use ports/adapters only at actual I/O or platform boundaries; do not introduce DDD, CQRS, saga, or service frameworks without a concrete, tested need. Documentation-only changes do not need artificial tests.

For SQLite changes, use temporary databases and test migration/atomicity behavior; never test against the user's database. For MCP/Tauri/CLI contract changes, validate the exposed schema, bounds, result shape, errors/refusals, availability and docs against the actual handler and focused tests. For performance claims, capture a representative baseline and repeat the same workload after the change; otherwise report that performance was not measured.

## Rule changes

When a rule or the planner changes, check it on fixtures: `tidymac_fixture_create {template: "smart_care_home"}` (or `edge_cases`), scan `module:smart_care`, `tidymac_plan {ticked: "default"}`, `tidymac_clean`, `tidymac_verify_fixture`. `unexpected_moved` means something was offered that must stay; `unexpectedly_kept` means a default tick went missing. If the template's expectation itself must change, edit `crates/core/src/fixtures/templates.rs` and say why in its `why` text.

## Reporting

Report the actual tool results (pass/fail, counts, the failures you fixed), what you skipped, and anything only checkable by hand. Never claim green without a passing `run_checks all` and `selftest` in this session.

